L000334	\section{Hardcore-gluon qudit model with Background charges}
L000335	To map the Kogut--Susskind $\mathrm{SU}(2)$ lattice Yang--Mills Hamiltonian of Eq.(1) to the qudit model used in numerical simulations, we:
L000336	\begin{enumerate*}
L000337	    \item perform a hardcore-gluon truncation of the $\mathrm{SU}(2)$ gauge fields \cite{Cataldi2024Simulating2+1DSU2};
L000338	    \item build the gauge-singlet local dressed basis accounting for the presence of eventual background charges; and
L000339	    \item gauge-defermionize.
L000340	\end{enumerate*}
L000341	The procedure is conveniently formulated by decomposing matter and gauging each Hilbert space in $\mathrm{SU}(2)$ irreducible representations (irreps).
L000342	This preliminary step already appears in the bases introduced in the main text: for gauge links, we adopt the irrep basis $\ket{\spin, \mL, \mR}$; for matter sites, the Fock basis
L000343	\begin{math}
L000344	    \{\ket{q}\}
L000345	    =
L000346	    \{
L000347	    {\ket{0}},\,
L000348	    {\ket{\rla},\ket{\gla} = \hpsi^{\dagger}_{\rla,\gla}\ket{0}},\,
L000349	    {\ket{2}    = \hpsi^{\dagger}_{\rla} \hpsi^{\dagger}_{\gla}\ket{0}}
L000350	    \}
L000351	\end{math}.
L000352	Matter basis states are associated with the following spin labels $(j,m)$:
L000353	\begin{equation}
L000354	    \ket{0}               \leftrightarrow (0,0)
L000355	    \,\quad
L000356	    \ket{\rla},\ket{\gla} \leftrightarrow (1/2,\pm1/2)
L000357	    \,\quad
L000358	    \ket{2}               \leftrightarrow (0,0)
L000359	    \,.
L000360	\end{equation}
L000361	Irrep decomposition specifies how local gauge rotations act on a site $\site$ and its neighboring links.
L000362	The generators of the infinitesimal rotations read $\forall \nu\in \{x,y,z\}$:
L000363	\begin{equation}
L000364	    \hat{G}^{\nu}_{\site}=\hat{R}_{\site-1,\site}^{\nu} + \hat{Q}_{\site}^{\nu}+\hat{L}_{\site,\site+1}^{\nu}\,,
L000365	\end{equation}
L000366	where
L000367	\begin{math}
L000368	    \hat{Q}^{\nu}_{\site}{=}
L000369	    \sum_{\alpha\beta}
L000370	    \hpsi^{\dagger}_{\site,\alpha}
L000371	    S^{(1/2)\nu}_{\alpha\beta}
L000372	    \hpsi_{\site,\beta}
L000373	\end{math}
L000374	rotates the quark field at $\site$, while $\hat{R}_{\site-1,\site}^{\nu}$ and $\hat{L}^{\nu}_{\site,n+1}$ account for the transformation of the gauge links at its left and right \cite{Zohar2015QuantumSimulationsLattice}:
L000375	\begin{align}
L000376	    \label{eq_LR_rishon_rotations}
L000377	    \mel{\spin^{\prime} \mL^{\prime} \mR^{\prime}}{\hat{L}^{\nu}}{ j \mL \mR } & =\delta_{\spin,\spin^{\prime}}S^{(j)\nu}_{\mL^{\prime},\mL} \delta_{\mR^{\prime},\mR}\,, \\
L000378	    \mel{\spin^{\prime} \mL^{\prime} \mR^{\prime} }{\hat{R}^{\nu}}{j \mL \mR } & =\delta_{\spin,\spin^{\prime}}\delta_{\mL^{\prime},\mL} S^{(j)\nu}_{\mR^{\prime},\mR}\,;
L000379	\end{align}
L000380	where $S^{(j)\nu}$ are the spin-$\spin$ $\mathfrak{su}(2)$ matrices.
L000381	From these operators, we can build the chromoelectric energy operator
L000382	\begin{math}
L000383	    \hat{E}^2 =\sum_{\nu}(\hat{R}^{\nu})^2 =\sum_{\nu}(\hat{L}^{\nu})^2
L000384	\end{math},
L000385	\idest{}, quadratic Casimir \cite{Zohar2015QuantumSimulationsLattice}:
L000386	\begin{align}
L000387	    \hat{E}^2\ket{j \mL \mR } & =j(j+1)\ket{j \mL \mR }\,.
L000388	\end{align}
L000389	Correspondingly, the action of the parallel transporter is given in terms of Clebsch-Gordan coefficients \cite{Zohar2015FormulationLatticeGauge}:
L000390	\begin{equation}
L000391	    \label{eq_parallel_transporter}
L000392	    \mel{\spin^{\prime}\mL^{\prime}\mR^{\prime}}{\hat{U}^{\alpha\beta}}{\spin\mL \mR}
L000393	    =\sqrt{\frac{2\spin+1}{2\spin ^{\mathrlap{\prime}}+1}}\:
L000394	    \overline{C^{\spin,\mL}_{\jonehalf,\alpha;\,\spin^{\prime},\mL^{\prime}}}
L000395	    C^{\spin^{\prime},\mR^{\prime}}_{\jonehalf,\beta;\,\spin,\mR}.
L000396	\end{equation}
L000397	Then, at each site $\site$, the $\mathrm{SU}(2)$ Gauss law reads:
L000398	\begin{equation}
L000399	    \hat{G}_{\site}^{\nu}
L000400	    \ket{\Psi_{\qty{b^{\kappa}_{n}}}} =
L000401	    b_{\site}^{\nu,\kappa}
L000402	    \ket{\Psi_{\qty{\vb{b}^{\kappa}_{\site}}}}, \; \forall \site, \nu,\kappa,
L000403	\end{equation}
L000404	where $b_{\site}^{\nu,\kappa}$ are the static background-charges present on the lattice site $\site$ of a QMB state with a specific $\kappa$ \emph{super-selection sector}, \idest{}, a specific lattice configuration of background charges $\qty[b^{\kappa}_{1}\dots b^{\kappa}_{N}]$ (where for simplicity we omit the spin-component index $\nu$).
L000405	
L000406	\subsection{Hardcore-gluon and background charge truncations}
L000407	In principle, the gauge field and the static background charges could occupy arbitrarily high spin shells.
L000408	To deal with a finite gauge-link Hilbert space, we perform the \emph{hardcore-gluon} approximation, which bounds $\spin\in\{0,1/2\}$ and yields an energy cutoff $\norm*{\hat{E}^2} \leq 3/4$ on the Casimir spectrum ($\norm{\:\cdot\:}$ denotes the matrix norm) \cite{Cataldi2024Simulating2+1DSU2, Calajo2024DigitalQuantumSimulation, Calajo2025QuantumManybodyScarring}.
L000409	A similar truncation is performed on the irreps of the static background charges $(\spin_{b},m_{b})$ where $\spin_{b}\in\{0,1/2\}$.
L000410	
L000411	Recovering the untruncated gauge group is \emph{not} essential for the present investigation.
L000412	The hardcore-gluon approximation faithfully captures the relevant low-energy physics in the strong-coupling regime $g_{0} \gg 1$, where the chromoelectric energy dominates the Hamiltonian.
L000413	Remarkably, this is precisely the regime where the distinctive DFL behavior emerges.
L000414	Similarly, restricting the background charges to the lowest two spin irreps is already sufficient to resolve the onset and structure of DFL.
L000415	
L000416	\subsection{Dressed-site basis with background charges}
L000417	Regardless of the gauge-field and the background-charge truncations, we can always observe that $\hat{L}^{\nu}$ and $\hat{R}^{\nu}$ from \cref{eq_LR_rishon_rotations} act nontrivially only on the $\mL$ and $\mR$ index, respectively.
L000418	We can then factorize each gauge link into a pair of new rishon degrees of freedom that live at its edges \cite{Silvi2014LatticeGaugeTensor}.
L000419	The basis states of each rishon mode are labeled as
L000420	\begin{math}
L000421	    \ket{0}\!=\!\ket{j\,{=}\,0,m\,{=}\,0}
L000422	\end{math}
L000423	and
L000424	\begin{math}
L000425	    \ket{\rla},
L000426	    \ket{\gla}\!=\!\ket{j\,{=}\,\frac{1}{2},m\,{=}\,\pm\frac{1}{2}}
L000427	\end{math}.
L000428	Combining each matter site with its two adjacent (left and right) gauge-rishons and the attached background charge, we forge a composite dressed-site with a 13-dimensional single dressed-site Hilbert space
L000429	\begin{math}
L000430	    \{
L000431	    \ket{\ell}{=}
L000432	    \ket{b^{\ell}_{\site}}\ket{\mR^{\ell}({n-1,n}),\,q^{\ell}(\site),\,\mL^{\ell}({\site,\site+1})}
L000433	    \}_{\ell=1}^{13}
L000434	\end{math}
L000435	where Gauss Law becomes an internal constraint \cite{Cataldi2024Simulating2+1DSU2}.
L000436	The first 6 basis states belong to the gauge-invariant sector with zero background-charge $b_{0}{=}\ket{\spin_{b}=0,m_{b}=0}$ \cite{Calajo2024DigitalQuantumSimulation, Calajo2025QuantumManybodyScarring}:
L000437	\begin{equation}
L000438	    \label{eq_extended_basis1}
L000439	    \begin{aligned}
L000440	        \ket{1} & \!=\!\ket{b_{0}}\!\ket{0,0,0},                                              &
L000441	        \ket{2} & \!=\!\ket{b_{0}}\!\frac{\ket{\rla,0,\gla}\!-\!\ket{\gla,0,\rla}}{\sqrt{2}},   \\
L000442	        \ket{3} & \!=\!\ket{b_{0}}\!\frac{\ket{0,\rla,\gla}\!-\!\ket{0,\gla,\rla}}{\sqrt{2}}, &
L000443	        \ket{4} & \!=\!\ket{b_{0}}\!\frac{\ket{\gla,\rla,0}\!-\!\ket{\rla,\gla,0}}{\sqrt{2}},   \\
L000444	        \ket{5} & \!=\!\ket{b_{0}}\!\ket{0,2,0},                                              &
L000445	        \ket{6} & \!=\!\ket{b_{0}}\!\frac{\ket{\rla,2,\gla}\!-\!\ket{\gla,2,\rla}}{\sqrt{2}},
L000446	    \end{aligned}
L000447	\end{equation}
L000448	while the remaining 7 states belong to the sector with finite background-charge $\qty{b_{\rla},b_{\gla}}{=}\ket{\spin_{b}=1/2,m_{b}=\pm1/2}$:
L000449	\begin{equation}
L000450	    \label{eq_extended_basis2}
L000451	    \begin{aligned}
L000452	        \ket{7}  & =\frac{\ket{b_{\rla}}\ket{0,0,\gla}\!-\!\ket{b_{\gla}}\ket{0,0,\rla}}{\sqrt{2}},  \\
L000453	        \ket{8}  & =\frac{\ket{b_{\rla}}\ket{\gla,0,0}\!-\!\ket{b_{\gla}}\ket{\rla,0,0}}{\sqrt{2}},  \\
L000454	        \ket{9}  & =\frac{\ket{b_{\rla}}\ket{0,\gla,0}\!-\!\ket{b_{\gla}}\ket{0,\rla,0}}{\sqrt{2}},  \\
L000455	        \ket{10} & =\frac{
L000456	            \ket{b_{\rla}}\qty[\ket{\gla,\rla,\gla}
L000457	                {-}\ket{\gla,\gla,\rla}]
L000458	            {-}\ket{b_{\gla}}\qty[\ket{\rla,\rla,\gla}
L000459	                {-}\ket{\rla,\gla,\rla}]
L000460	        }{2},                                                                                        \\
L000461	        \ket{11} & =\frac{
L000462	            \ket{b_{\rla}}\ket{\rla,\gla,\gla}
L000463	        +\ket{b_{\gla}}\ket{\gla,\rla,\rla}}{\sqrt{3}}                                               \\
L000464	                 & -\frac{
L000465	            \ket{b_{\rla}}\qty[\ket{\gla,\rla,\gla}{+}\ket{\gla,\gla,\rla}]{+}
L000466	            \ket{b_{\gla}}\qty[\ket{\rla,\rla,\gla}{+}\ket{\rla,\gla,\rla}]
L000467	        }{2\sqrt{3}},                                                                                \\
L000468	        \ket{12} & =\frac{\ket{b_{\rla}}\ket{0,2,\gla}\!-\!\ket{b_{\gla}}\ket{0,2,\rla}}{\sqrt{2}},  \\
L000469	        \ket{13} & =\frac{\ket{b_{\rla}}\ket{\gla,2,0}\!-\!\ket{b_{\gla}}\ket{\rla,2,0}}{\sqrt{2}} .
L000470	    \end{aligned}
L000471	\end{equation}
L000472	Notice that, since $\forall \site$, $[\hat{G}_{\site}-b_{\site},\ham]=0$, the two background-charge sectors of \cref{eq_extended_basis1,eq_extended_basis2} are completely decoupled and do not interact with each other.
L000473	Therefore, any superselection sector $\kappa$, \idest{} lattice configuration of local (single-site) background charges $\qty{b^{\kappa}_{n}}=\qty[b^{\kappa}_{1}\dots b^{\kappa}_{N}]$ is independent of the others $\{b^{\kappa^{\prime}}_{n}\}$ and can be simulated independently.
L000474	
L000475	\subsection{Gauge defermionization}
L000476	The dressed-site formalism introduced previously is equivalent to the original LGT description, provided that it recovers the original physical space.
L000477	Namely, it requires that the left and right rishons on each link must be in the same spin shell $\spin$ by restricting to the even parity sector of a $\mathbb{Z}_2$ symmetry, whose action reads:
L000478	\begin{equation}
L000479	    \label{eq_link_symmetry}
L000480	    \hat{P}= +\op{0} - (\op{\rla} + \op{\gla})
L000481	    \,.
L000482	\end{equation}
L000483	Correspondingly, the parallel transporter in \cref{eq_parallel_transporter} can be written as:
L000484	\begin{equation}
L000485	    \label{eq_parallel_transporter_rishons}
L000486	    \hat{U}^{\alpha\beta}_{\site,n+1} \to \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
L000487	    \rishon^{\mathrm{(L)} \alpha}_{\site,n+1}
L000488	    (\rishon^{\mathrm{(R)} \beta}_{\site,n+1})^\dagger
L000489	    \,,
L000490	\end{equation}
L000491	where the rishon modes read:
L000492	\begin{equation}
L000493	    \rishon^{\rla} = \ketbra{0}{\rla} + \ketbra{\gla}{0}
L000494	    \,,\quad
L000495	    \rishon^{\gla} = \ketbra{0}{\gla} - \ketbra{\rla}{0}
L000496	    \,.
L000497	\end{equation}
L000498	Observe that the right-hand side of \cref{eq_parallel_transporter_rishons} preserves the link parity in \cref{eq_link_symmetry}, as desired.
L000499	Conversely, a single rishon operator always inverts it:
L000500	\begin{math}
L000501	    \acomm*{\hat{P}}{\rishon^{\alpha}} = 0
L000502	\end{math}.
L000503	The existence of a $\mathbb{Z}_2$-symmetry in the rishon spaces allows to gauge-defermionize the model \cite{Cataldi2024Simulating2+1DSU2, Ballarin2024DigitalQuantumSimulation}:
L000504	we take $\rishon^{\alpha}$ to be fermionic and rewrite the Hamiltonian in Eq.(1) in terms of the following bosonic operators:
L000505	\begin{equation}
L000506	    \begin{aligned}
L000507	        \hat{Q}_{\site}^{\mathrm{(L,R)}} & = \sum_{\alpha} (\rishon^{\mathrm{(L,R)} \alpha}_{\site,\site+1})^\dagger \hpsi_{\site,\alpha}
L000508	        \,,                                                                                                                               \\
L000509	        \hat{M}_{\site}                  & =\sum_{\alpha} \hpsi_{\site,\alpha}^\dagger\hpsi_{\site,\alpha},                               \\
L000510	        \hat{C}_{\site}                  & =\frac{1}{2}\sum_{\nu}\qty[\hat{R}^{\nu,2}_{\site-1, \site}+\hat{L}^{\nu,2}_{\site, \site+1}]
L000511	        \,.
L000512	    \end{aligned}
L000513	\end{equation}
L000514	For potential quantum simulation implementations, it is convenient to rewrite these solely in terms of Hermitian operators, introducing \cite{Calajo2024DigitalQuantumSimulation}:
L000515	\begin{equation}
L000516	    \begin{aligned}
L000517	        \hat{A}^{(1)} & \mathbin=\hat{Q}^{\mathrm{(L)}} \mathop+ \hat{Q}^{\mathrm{(L)}\dagger}\,,                 &
L000518	        \hat{B}^{(1)} & \mathbin=\hat{Q}^{\mathrm{(R)}} \mathop+ \hat{Q}^{\mathrm{(R)}\dagger}\,,                   \\
L000519	        \hat{A}^{(2)} & \mathbin= i \big[ \hat{Q}^{\mathrm{(L)}} \mathop- \hat{Q}^{\mathrm{(L)}\dagger} \big] \,, &
L000520	        \hat{B}^{(2)} & \mathbin= i \big[ \hat{Q}^{\mathrm{(R)}} \mathop- \hat{Q}^{\mathrm{(R)}\dagger} \big] \,.
L000521	    \end{aligned}
L000522	\end{equation}
L000523	Finally, we map Eq.(1) to the following 13-dimensional (6 with no background charge + 7 with finite background charge) qudit Hamiltonian \cite{Calajo2024DigitalQuantumSimulation}:
L000524	\begin{equation}
L000525	    \label{eq_Heff}
L000526	    \ham
L000527	    = \sum_{\site,p} \hat{A}^{(p)}_{\site} \hat{B}^{(p)}_{\site+1}
L000528	    + \mass \sum_{\site}(-1)^{\site} \hat{M}_{\site}
L000529	    + g^2 \sum_{\site} \hat{C}_{\site}\,,
L000530	\end{equation}
L000531	where we rescaled energy to absorb the hopping pre-factor, $\ham=4\sqrt{2}\lspace\ham_{0}$, and defined
L000532	the dimensionless couplings $m=4\sqrt{2}\lspace \mass[0]$, and $\coupling^{2}=\frac{3\sqrt{2}}{4}\lspace\coupling[0]^2$.
L000533	
L000534	\subsection{Particle populations}
L000535	Within this dressed-site basis, we can still easily measure various particle-density operators, which yield information about the physical processes occurring during the dynamics.
L000536	In particular, we can define:
L000537	\begin{subequations}
L000538	    \begin{align}
L000539	        \hat{\rho}_{\site}^{[2]} &
L000540	        =\hpsi_{\site,\rla}^{\dagger}
L000541	        \hpsi_{\site,\rla}
L000542	        \hpsi_{\site,\gla}^{\dagger}
L000543	        \hpsi_{\site,\gla}                         \\
L000544	        \hat{\rho}_{\site}^{[1]} &
L000545	        =\hat{M}_{\site}-2\hat{\rho}_{\site}^{[2]} \\
L000546	        \hat{\rho}_{\site}^{[0]} &
L000547	        =1-\hat{\rho}^{[1]}_{\site}-\hat{\rho}_{\site}^{[2]},
L000548	    \end{align}
L000549	\end{subequations}
L000550	where $\hat{\rho}_{\site}^{[0]}$, $\hat{\rho}_{\site}^{[1]}$, and $\hat{\rho}_{\site}^{[2]}$ measure the single-site zero, one, and double occupancy respectively.
L000551	Correspondingly, within the adopted staggered fermion solution \cite{Susskind1977LatticeFermions}, these single-site observables allow us to compute true-particle quantities like the average (anti-)baryon, meson, and vacuum densities defined in Eq.(8) of the main text, which explicitly read:
L000552	\begin{subequations}
L000553	    \begin{align}
L000554	        \hat{p}_2 & =\frac{1}{\Nsites}\sum_{\site}\qty[
L000555	            \frac{1+(-1)^{\site}}{2}\hat{\rho}_{\site}^{[2]}+
L000556	        \frac{1-(-1)^{\site}}{2}\hat{\rho}_{\site}^{[0]}],                  \\
L000557	        \hat{p}_1 & =\frac{1}{\Nsites}\sum_{\site}\hat{\rho}_{\site}^{[1]}, \\
L000558	        \hat{p}_0 & =\frac{1}{\Nsites}\sum_{\site}\qty[
L000559	            \frac{1-(-1)^{\site}}{2}\hat{\rho}_{\site}^{[2]}+
L000560	            \frac{1+(-1)^{\site}}{2}\hat{\rho}_{\site}^{[0]}].
L000561	    \end{align}
L000562	    \label{eq_particle_densities}
L000563	\end{subequations}
L000564	
L000565	\section{Initial state candidates for dynamics}\label{sec_intial_states}
L000566	To observe DFL, we start the dynamics with an equal superposition of all the possible configurations of single-site background charges, \idest{}, a superposition of all the $\mathcal{N}_{\mathrm{SS}}$ \emph{superselection sectors} $\qty{b^{\kappa}_{n}}=\qty[b^{\kappa}_{1}\dots b^{\kappa}_{N}]_{\kappa=1}^{\mathcal{N}_{\mathrm{SS}}}$, as in Eq.(3).
L000567	The explicit construction of candidates for initial states within the proposed dressed-site formalism requires that the left- and right-gauge irreps of each link share the same Casimir, as imposed in \cref{eq_link_symmetry}.
L000568	Dealing with staggered fermions \cite{Susskind1977LatticeFermions}, finite mass coupling values $m$ will favor configurations where even and odd sites host a complementary state of matter fields (vacuum and pair) respectively.
L000569	Therefore, good candidates for the initial state consist of staggered matter configurations where blocks of \(x\) empty (e) sites ($e_{1},\!\dots, e_{x}$) alternate with blocks of \(x\) doubly occupied (o) sites ($o_{1},\dots, o_{x}$).
L000570	These states have an initial matter imbalance, defined in Eq.(5), equal to 1, and their time evolution allows localization to be detected.
L000571	
L000572	Practically, empty lattice sites are in a superposition of the states $\ket{1}$, $\ket{2}$, $\ket{7}$, and $\ket{8}$ of \cref{eq_extended_basis1,eq_extended_basis2} without matter fields.
L000573	Correspondingly, as for doubly occupied sites, we consider a superposition of the states $\ket{5}$, $\ket{6}$, $\ket{12}$, and $\ket{13}$ of \cref{eq_extended_basis1,eq_extended_basis2}.
L000574	According to the link symmetries in \cref{eq_link_symmetry}, we discard all superpositions in which neighboring site states do not share the same gauge irrep on the attached semi-links.
L000575	Any staggered initial (BG) state is then constructed with the following rules:
L000576	\begin{equation}
L000577	    \label{eq_superposition_rules}
L000578	    \begin{split}
L000579	        \ket{1}_{e_{1}}  & \otimes[\ket{1}_{e_{2}}+\ket{7}_{e_{2}}]\dots
L000580	        \ket{1}_{e_{x}}  \otimes[\ket{5}_{o_{1}}+\ket{12}_{o_{1}}]         \\
L000581	        \ket{2}_{e_{1}}  & \otimes[\ket{2}_{e_{2}}+\ket{8}_{e_{2}}]\dots
L000582	        \ket{2}_{e_{x}}  \otimes[\ket{6}_{o_{1}}+\ket{13}_{o_{1}}]         \\
L000583	        \ket{7}_{e_{1}}  & \otimes[\ket{2}_{e_{2}}+\ket{8}_{e_{2}}]\dots
L000584	        \ket{7}_{e_{x}}  \otimes[\ket{6}_{o_{1}}+\ket{13}_{o_{1}}]         \\
L000585	        \ket{8}_{e_{1}}  & \otimes[\ket{1}_{e_{2}}+\ket{7}_{e_{2}}]\dots
L000586	        \ket{8}_{e_{x}}  \otimes[\ket{5}_{o_{1}}+\ket{12}_{o_{1}}]         \\
L000587	        \\
L000588	        \ket{5}_{o_{1}}  & \otimes[\ket{5}_{o_{2}}+\ket{12}_{o_{2}}] \dots
L000589	        \ket{5}_{o_{x}}  \otimes[\ket{1}_{e_{1}}+\ket{7}_{e_{1}}]          \\
L000590	        \ket{6}_{o_{1}}  & \otimes[\ket{6}_{o_{2}}+\ket{13}_{o_{2}}] \dots
L000591	        \ket{6}_{o_{x}}  \otimes[\ket{2}_{e_{1}}+\ket{8}_{e_{1}}]          \\
L000592	        \ket{12}_{o_{1}} & \otimes[\ket{6}_{o_{2}}+\ket{13}_{o_{2}}] \dots
L000593	        \ket{12}_{o_{x}}  \otimes[\ket{2}_{e_{1}}+\ket{8}_{e_{1}}]         \\
L000594	        \ket{13}_{o_{1}} & \otimes[\ket{5}_{o_{2}}+\ket{12}_{o_{2}}] \dots
L000595	        \ket{13}_{o_{x}}  \otimes[\ket{1}_{e_{1}}+\ket{7}_{e_{1}}]
L000596	    \end{split}
L000597	\end{equation}
L000598	The simplest nontrivial staggered superposition of \emph{superselection sectors}  corresponds to $x=2$, where matter sites are in a sequence of two fully \emph{empty} ($e_{1}$ and $e_{2}$) and two fully \emph{occupied} sites ($o_{1}$ and $o_{2}$).
L000599	Such a configuration corresponds to the state defined in Eq.(3) and is used in all numerical simulations discussed in the main text.
L000600	
L000601	Similar results can be obtained by choosing $x$ as any exact divisor of $N/2$, up to considering the half-chain domain wall configuration with empty sites ($e_{1} \dots e_{N/2}$) and the other one with fully occupied sites ($o_{N/2+1} \dots o_{N}$).
L000602	The main challenge of the latter state is its long relaxation timescale, which may strain numerical resources.
L000603	
L000604	\subsection{MPS representation of the initial state}
L000605	In exact diagonalization, for an $N$-site lattice chain with periodic boundary conditions (PBC), any of these staggered initial states of the dynamics is made of a superposition of $4\cdot 2^{N-1}$ lattice configurations compatible with \cref{eq_extended_basis1,eq_extended_basis2}.
L000606	Correspondingly, the same superposition can be written as an exact matrix product state (MPS) of the form \cite{Fannes1992FinitelyCorrelatedStates, Klumper1991EquivalenceSolutionAnisotropic, Klumper1993MatrixProductGround}:
L000607	\begin{equation}
L000608	    \ket{\psi}=\sum_{i_1,..i_N} \mathrm{Tr} \left [ A^{(i_1)}_{1} A^{(i_2)}_{2}...A^{(i_N)}_{N} \right ] \ket{i_1,i_2,..i_N},
L000609	\end{equation}
L000610	where each matrix $A^{(i_{k})}_{k}$ has dimension $\chi_{k-1}{\times}\chi_{k}$ apart from the two edges, $\chi_{1}=1$ and $\chi_{N}=1$.
L000611	Under PBC, the superposition state of superselection sectors in Eq.(3) can be written as an MPS with bond dimension $\chi=4$ where the occupied sites take the form:
L000612	\begin{equation}
L000613	    A^{(i_{k})}_{\rm o}=\begin{pmatrix}
L000614	        \ket{5}      & \ket{12} & 0            & 0        \\
L000615	        \;\;\ket{13} & \ket{6}  & 0            & 0        \\
L000616	        0            & 0        & \ket{5}      & \ket{12} \\
L000617	        0            & 0        & \;\;\ket{13} & \ket{6}
L000618	    \end{pmatrix}\,,
L000619	\end{equation}
L000620	while for the empty sites
L000621	\begin{equation}
L000622	    A^{(i_{k})}_{\rm e}=\begin{pmatrix}
L000623	        \ket{1} & \ket{7} & 0       & 0       \\
L000624	        \ket{8} & \ket{2} & 0       & 0       \\
L000625	        0       & 0       & \ket{1} & \ket{7} \\
L000626	        0       & 0       & \ket{8} & \ket{2}
L000627	    \end{pmatrix}\,.
L000628	\end{equation}
L000629	The two edges ($\site=1,N$) read instead:
L000630	\begin{equation}
L000631	    A^{(i_{1(N)})}_{\rm o}=\begin{pmatrix}
L000632	        \ket{5}  \\
L000633	        \ket{12} \\
L000634	        \ket{13} \\
L000635	        \ket{6}
L000636	    \end{pmatrix} \quad\quad A^{(i_{1(N)})}_{\rm e}=\begin{pmatrix}
L000637	        \ket{1} \\
L000638	        \ket{8} \\
L000639	        \ket{7} \\
L000640	        \ket{2}
L000641	    \end{pmatrix}.
L000642	\end{equation}
L000643	This representation explains the initial value of the entanglement entropy in FIG.4 of the main text.
