L000670	\section{Thermal and long-time predictions}
L000671	\label{app_ensembles}
L000672	To clearly distinguish between the thermal and nonthermal behavior of the model under consideration, we compare the very long-time prediction with the one from the microcanonical ensemble.
L000673	For a unitary dynamics of a generic initial state expressed in the Hamiltonian eigenbasis, $\ket{\Psi(0)}=\sum_{s}C_{s}\ket{\Phi_{s}}$, the long-time behavior of a generic observable $\hat{O}$ corresponds to the expectation value predicted by the \emph{diagonal} ensemble (DE) \cite{Rigol2008ThermalizationItsMechanism}:
L000674	\begin{equation}
L000675	    \begin{split}
L000676	        \bar{O} & \equiv\lim_{T\to\infty} \frac{1}{T}\int_0^T dt \braket{\Psi(t)|\hat O|\Psi(t)}                                           \\
L000677	                & =\lim_{T\to\infty} \frac{1}{T}\int_0^T dt
L000678	        \sum_{s,p} C^{*}_{s}C_{p}\braket{\Phi_{s}|e^{it\hat{H}}\hat Oe^{-it\hat{H}}|\Phi_{p}}                                              \\
L000679	                & =\sum_{s,p} C^{*}_{s}C_{p}\braket{\Phi_{s}|\hat O|\Phi_{p}}\lim_{T\to\infty} \frac{1}{T}\int_0^T e^{i(E_{s}-E_{p})t} dt.
L000680	    \end{split}
L000681	    \label{eq_DE}
L000682	\end{equation}
L000683	If the Hamiltonian has no degeneracies, the oscillatory phases $e^{i(E_s-E_p)t}$ dephase for $s\neq p$, yielding:
L000684	\begin{equation}
L000685	    \bar{O}=\sum_{s}\abs{C_{s}}^2 \braket{\Phi_s|\hat O|\Phi_s}
L000686	    \equiv \Tr\{\hat O\, \hat{\varrho}_{\rm DE}\},
L000687	    \label{eq_nondegenerate_DE}
L000688	\end{equation}
L000689	where $\hat{\varrho}_{DE}=\sum_{s}\abs{C_s}^2 \ketbra{\Phi_s}$.
L000690	If instead the spectrum contains degenerate energy subspaces $\mathcal{H}_\alpha$ with projectors $\hat P_\alpha$, the long-time average retains all matrix elements within each degenerate block:
L000691	\begin{equation}
L000692	    \bar{O}=\sum_{\alpha}\Tr\left(\hat{P}_{\alpha}\, \ketbra{\Psi(0)}{\Psi(0)}\hat{P}_\alpha\right)\hat{O}.
L000693	    \label{eq_degenerate_DE}
L000694	\end{equation}
L000695	Correspondingly, the DE becomes block-diagonal
L000696	\begin{equation}
L000697	    \hat{\varrho}_{\rm DE}=\sum_{\alpha}\hat{P}_{\alpha}, \ketbra{\Psi(0)}{\Psi(0)}\hat{P}_{\alpha},
L000698	\end{equation}
L000699	and reduces to the usual diagonal form only when all energies are non-degenerate.
L000700	
L000701	In any case, if the expectation value of the observable $\braket{\hat{O}(t)}$ approaches the long-time average $\braket{\hat{O}}_{DE}$ over long times, the system \emph{equilibrates}.
L000702	\begin{figure}
L000703	    \includegraphics[width=1\columnwidth]{figures/figS2_fragmentation_spectrum.pdf}
L000704	    \caption{\textbf{Spectral properties of Fragmentation.}
L000705	        (left) Many-body spectrum analysis for Eq.(1) at $m\!=\!10$ and $g^{2}\!=\!2$: (top) the overlap $\mathcal{O}\!=\!\abs*{\braket{\Psi(0)|\Phi_s}}^2$ between the initial state in Eq.(4) and the spectrum eigenstates; (bottom) the corresponding half-chain bipartite entanglement entropy for each eigenstate of the spectrum.
L000706	        (right) Time evolution of the initial state in Eq.(4): (top) measure of the fidelity with the initial state and (bottom) half-chain bipartite entanglement entropy.}
L000707	    \label{fig_fragmentation_spectrum}
L000708	\end{figure}
L000709	Correspondingly, the expected thermal behavior can be computed with the microcanonical ensemble average (ME)
L000710	\begin{equation}
L000711	    \braket{\hat{O}}_{\rm{ME}}=\Tr\{\hat{O}\hat{\varrho}_{\rm{ME}} \}\,,
L000712	\end{equation}
L000713	where the microcanonical state is defined as a superposition within the energy shell $[E_\text{q}\!-\!\delta E, E_\text{q}\!+\!\delta E]$, with the initial-state energy $E_\text{q}\!=\!\bra{\Psi(0)}\hat{H}\ket{\Psi(0)}$, containing $N_{E,\delta E }$ eigenstates:
L000714	\begin{equation}
L000715	    \label{eq_microcanonical_state}
L000716	    \hat{\varrho}_{\rm{ME}}=\frac{1}{N_{E,\delta E }}
L000717	    \sum_{\substack{s;\\|E_s-E_\text{q}|<\delta E}}\ketbra{\Phi_s}{\Phi_s}\,.
L000718	\end{equation}
L000719	Whenever the ME and DE predictions of generic observables coincide, the system, quenched on the chosen initial state, \emph{thermalizes}.
L000720	Conversely, it exhibits nonthermal behavior due to Hilbert-space fragmentation, scarring dynamics, or other exotic phenomena.
L000721	
L000722	In the superposition of superselection sectors in Eq.(2), DE and ME predictions of the imbalance in Eq.(5) (and any other observable) are obtained as classical averages of the DE and ME values of each background-charge configuration.
L000723	Namely, we have:
L000724	\begin{equation}
L000725	    \braket{\hat{O}}_{\rm{ME/DE}}^{\rm{BG}}=\frac{1}{\mathcal{N}_{ss}}\sum_{\kappa=1}^{\mathcal{N}_{ss}}
L000726	    \braket{\hat{O}}_{\rm{ME/DE}}^{\qty[b^{\kappa}_{1}\dots b^{\kappa}_{N}]}
L000727	    \label{eq_average_sector}
L000728	\end{equation}
