L000761	\section{Ergodic entanglement entropy scaling in the gauge invariant sector}
L000762	\label{sec_entanglement_GI}
L000763	In an ergodic regime, we expect quantum correlations to spread \emph{linearly} in time \cite{Kaufman2016QuantumThermalizationEntanglement}.
L000764	This is exactly what we observe for the GI sector across all coupling regimes compatible with ergodicity.
L000765	In \cref{fig_entropy_GI}, we display the bipartite entanglement entropy scaling within the GI sector as a function of time.
L000766	For a direct comparison, we show the GI entanglement's linear scaling, alongside the SS \emph{logarithmic} scaling already shown in FIG.4 of the main text.
L000767	To mitigate the effect of finite-size oscillations, similarly to what has been done for the imbalance in FIG.2, we consider the time-averaged entanglement entropy $\overline{\mathcal{S}}(t)=\frac{1}{\Delta t}\int^{t+\Delta t/2}_{t-\Delta t/2}d\tau\,\mathcal{S}(\tau)$, for an intermediate time window $\Delta t=2.5$.
L000768	
L000769	As in the SS case discussed in FIG.4, large-$g^2$ couplings strongly penalize gauge excitations and suppress hopping processes.
L000770	The resulting constrained dynamics affects the spread of quantum correlations, yielding slower entanglement growth and lower saturation values than in the small-$g^{2}$ regime.
L000771	In addition to the thermal relaxation of local observables demonstrated in FIG.3, this confirms the ergodic nature of the GI sectors in the identified regimes of FIG.1.
